  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry through early evening, with another batch\nof rain expected overnight and Saturday (with the highest rain \ntotals across NE NC). Highs Saturday remain below normal. \n\nAfternoon WX analysis indicates a frontal boundary nearly stationary\nacross the SC and southern NC coastal plain, with westerly flow\naloft. A relatively weak upper level trough is in place from \nthe mid-MS Valley to the OH Valley, and is forecast to amplify \nto the west of the Appalachians later tonight, eventually dropping\nSE across the region on Saturday. The flow aloft looks to back \nslightly later tonight, to the WSW, helping to lift the moisture\nand precip back north into the local area. The latest models are\nin decent agreement bringing likely to categorical PoPs into \nsouthern VA and NE NC after midnight. The models also support \nraising PoPs to 40-60% as far north as metro RIC, though \nrainfall amounts will tend to average 0.10-0.20\" or less that \nfar to the north. QPF amounts over far southern VA and NE NC\nwill average 0.50\" to 1.00\" with some locally higher amounts\npossible in NE NC. Thunderstorms look quite limited north of \nthe VA- NC border, but will bring slight chances into far \nsouthern VA for in case a few elevated cells develop where the \nNAM/GFS depict ~200 J/Kg of MU CAPE late tonight into Sat aftn.\nRain chances and rather cool high temperatures continue \nSaturday, especially south. Lows tonight will mostly be in the \n60s with some lower 70s in NE NC and at the coast in SE VA. \nHighs Saturday will range from the mid to upper 70s across the \nsouth, with some lower 80s NE (where some partial sun is most \nlikely). Drying out Sat night as the upper trough moves \noffshore, with lows mainly in the 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with just isolated late day showers/storms\nSunday/Monday, with higher rain chances Tuesday into Wednesday;\nstrong to severe storms are possible Tuesday. \n\n\nWeak sfc high pressure is forecast to settle over the region\nSunday-Monday, bringing a return to more seasonable temperatures\nwith highs in the mid 80s to around 90F. Mainly dry outside of\nisolated to widely scattered late day convection. Storm chances\nincrease later Tuesday as a strong upper level trough dives SE \nfrom Canada and lingers across the NE and mid-Atlantic states \nthrough at least Wednesday. The best chance for widespread \nstorms appears to be late Tuesday afternoon/evening though with \na lot of uncertainty at this range. With the strength of trough,\nand even a potential sfc low <1000mb, there will be the \npotential for severe weather, especially if the timing lines up\nwith the greatest instability, and SPC Day 5 Outlook has the \nlocal area in a Slight Risk.",
